    {
      "ply": 20,
      "san": "Bxh6",
      "human_san": "Bxh6",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "The most accurate move for Black, though it leads to a slight advantage for White (+0.34, Stockfish:16) after [pv:Bxh6 Bg5 Bxg5 hxg5 Nc6]; in return, White's rooks may become more active on the open files further along that line, where rooks on open files often dominate. Capturing on d4 with cxd4 was clearly weaker, leaving White with a decisive advantage after [pv:cxd4 Qf4 dxc3 Bc7 cxb2]. The queens are off \u2014 the game enters its final phase, where technique matters more than tactics. The position stays balanced.",
      "system_symbol": "!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 21,
      "san": "Bg5",
      "human_san": "Bg5",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "Bg5 is the most accurate move, maintaining White's advantage (+0.32, Stockfish:16) by immediately targeting the bishop on h6. The continuation [pv:Bg5 Bxg5 hxg5 Nc6 Nb5] may open the h-file for White's rook, and an open h-file often amplifies rook activity. Bc7 leads to equality [pv:Bc7 Nc6 Nf3 Nb4 Kd1] and likewise targets the knight on b8, but Bg5 is the stronger choice that keeps White's edge.",
      "system_symbol": "!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
